    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d861">

                <pb facs="0944" n="932" xml:id="pg932"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">690D.116/3: Telegram</note>
                <head>
                    <hi rendition="#italic">The Chargé in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Murphy</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State </hi>
                </head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right">
                        <placeName>
                            <hi rendition="#small-caps">Paris</hi></placeName>, <date
                            calendar="gregorian" when="1940-02-09T12:00:00+00:00">February 9,
                            1940—noon</date>.<lb/>[Received February 9—9:42 a.m.]</dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>188. We left at the Foreign Office this morning an <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> along the lines of the Department’s air mail instruction
                    No. 1929 of January 25 and made as directed a general reservation with respect
                    to American treaty rights covering trade relations with those French colonies
                    and mandated areas to which the treaties under reference apply.</p>
                <p>Rageot<anchor n="45" xml:id="d861-f1"/> read the <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi> with evident perturbation. He said that all that the
                    Department pointed out was unquestionably true but that the French Government’s
                    attitude was dictated by wartime necessity which were overwhelming.</p>
                <p>Although Rageot promised that we would be provided at a later date, when an
                    opportunity had been afforded for a careful study of our <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >aide-mémoire</hi>, with a more comprehensive statement of the French
                    position, which could be reported to the Department, he ventured the informal
                    opinion that there could be no withdrawal from the policy which had been adopted
                    and that the probability of protest such as ours had been discounted in advance
                    of these various measures.</p>
                <closer>
                    <signed>
                        <hi rendition="#small-caps">Murphy</hi>
                    </signed>
                </closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d861-f1" n="45" xml:id="d861-f1-content">
                <p>Maxime Rageot, Assistant Chief of the Division of Near East and African Affairs,
                    French Ministry for Foreign Affairs.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
